{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"ambery-perilous-prawn","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Coaching move: when a plan stops CONVERGING, propose a decomposition instead of another finding list\n\n## The observation\n\nTwice in epic e369 a plan absorbed round after round of review findings, and both times the\nremedy was not a better answer to the findings — it was **splitting the ticket**:\n\n* **J9 [rebar:bda8-370d-23ad-4e36]** blocked EIGHT times. Extracting the exception contract into\n  J10 [rebar:bb8c...] produced a first-try PASS.\n* **b586 [rebar:b586-2fda-8a15-4260]** has now taken TEN rounds and is still BLOCK. Its findings\n  partition cleanly into two concerns that alternate: the POLICY (rounds 1, 4, 8, 9, 10 — helper\n  reuse, duplicate-risk scoping, citation precision, the per-call read/mutation boundary, what\n  exception escapes at cap exhaustion) and the MECHANICS of the module extraction the 800-LOC cap\n  forces (rounds 6, 7 — what moves, what routes through what, which tree the LOC is measured on).\n  Each fix in one concern re-opened surface in the other.\n\nThe gate is doing its job in both cases — the findings were real and several were serious. What it\ncannot currently say is the thing a senior reviewer would say on round four: *\"stop answering these\none at a time; this is two tickets.\"*\n\n## Why the obvious trigger is WRONG\n\n\"After N rounds, suggest splitting\" would have fired on b586 at round 3 and been wrong. Rounds 1\nthrough 9 CONVERGED — 20 advisory + 1 blocking, down to 1 blocking. That is a plan being iterated\nwell, and telling its author to restructure would have been noise that trains people to ignore the\ngate.\n\n**The signal is not the round count. It is the loss of convergence**, and it has a specific shape:\n\n1. **Non-monotonicity** — the blocking count RISES after having fallen (b586: 1 blocking at round 9,\n   7+ at round 10). That is the fingerprint of \"fixing one thing re-opened another.\"\n2. **Alternating clusters** — findings partition into >= 2 groups by criteria code and cited\n   location, and consecutive rounds hit DIFFERENT groups rather than drilling into one.\n3. **Disjoint citation sets** — the groups cite non-overlapping files/sections (b586: the extraction\n   findings cite `transport.py` structure; the policy findings cite `_errors` / `dispatch_one`\n   semantics).\n\nRequiring all three is what keeps it from firing on a plan that is simply deep.\n\n## The move itself\n\nWhen those conditions hold, the gate stops emitting an (N+1)th finding list and instead emits a\n**structural verdict**: the cluster partition it actually computed, round-by-round, plus a proposed\ndecomposition — \"these 10 rounds' findings fall into 2 groups that alternate; here are the 2\ncandidate tickets, and here is which findings belong to each.\"\n\nDesign constraints that matter:\n\n* **Advisory, not a new blocking verdict.** Keeping one ticket is a legitimate authorial choice; the\n  gate should make the structural claim VISIBLE, not enforce it. It also must not become a way to\n  dodge findings — the findings still stand and still block.\n* **It must show its work.** The value is in the partition, not the recommendation. An author who\n  disagrees needs the round-by-round cluster assignment to argue with, exactly as they would with a\n  finding's evidence.\n* **No new storage.** `rebar audit show <id>`'s `plan_reviews` array already retains the per-round\n  `REVIEW_RESULT` sidecars — verified: b586 currently carries 10 retained rounds. The trend is\n  computable from what is already there.\n* **Prefer deterministic clustering first.** Findings already carry structured `criteria` and\n  `location` fields, so grouping may not need an LLM call at all. Reach for a model only if the\n  deterministic partition proves too coarse.\n\n## The confound to handle honestly\n\nb586's description grew from ~8.8 KB to ~28 KB across the ten rounds. **More plan is more surface,\nso some of the rising finding count is the plan getting longer, not worse.** Any trend metric should\nnormalize by plan size (or at minimum surface the size delta alongside the count) — otherwise the\nmove will fire on thorough authors, which is precisely the population it should leave alone.\n\n## Open questions (this is an idea, not a design)\n\n* Do the retained `plan_reviews` sidecars expose per-round BLOCKING COUNTS? The projection checked\n  here showed the verdicts but not the counts, so the trend's raw input needs confirming before any\n  of this is buildable.\n* Where should it surface — in the `review-plan` output, in `review-plan --status`, or only in\n  `rebar audit show`? The first is loudest and the most likely to be resented.\n* Should it ever fire at round 1 on a plan that is obviously two tickets (e.g. one whose Scope lists\n  two unrelated modules and whose ACs partition with them)? That is a different, cheaper detector\n  and possibly the better first version.\n* J9's split was authored by a human noticing the pattern. Is one automated nudge enough to change\n  behaviour, or does the coaching only work when it names the CANDIDATE SPLIT concretely?\n\n## Provenance\n\nBoth instances are recorded in session log [rebar:558e-55ce-d9f1-4fa9] (handoff #4 lesson 3, and\nhandoff #7). The b586 round-by-round breakdown that this generalises from is a comment on\n[rebar:b586-2fda-8a15-4260].","id":"fa6e-5301-848a-4b00","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Plan-review coaching move: when findings stop converging, propose a decomposition"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785543639150041001,"uuid":"28a2d635-acd7-439d-8e82-42e5a92cbb2e"}